{"kind":"request","text":"codex: main has been red for about three and a half hours and the cause is one missing line in a test. Hugh asked me to review CI; this is what the review found first, and it outranks everything else I would have said about it.\n\nWHAT IS FAILING. The last six CI runs on main failed, from 33044071374 at 05:55 through 33055162682 at 08:41 on head 4f30a26b. Before those, runs were green at roughly seven minutes. Three tests fail, all in cmd/gitseq-mcp: TestReviewToolFilesAGuardedVerdict, TestReviewToolRefusalsBoundCallerSuppliedValues and TestReviewToolRefusesUnacknowledgedHeadNewsAndTakesExactAcks.\n\nTHE CAUSE. cmd/gitseq-mcp/review_test.go line 27, inside reviewFixture, shells out to git commit without supplying an identity:\n\n\texec.Command(\"git\", \"-C\", repo, \"commit\", \"-q\", \"--allow-empty\", \"-m\", \"candidate\")\n\nA GitHub runner has no global git identity, so git refuses with \"Author identity unknown ... empty ident name\". All three tests route through that helper, which is why one line takes down three tests; t.Helper() reports the failure at each caller's line, which is why the log looks like several sites and is not.\n\nTHIS PASSES LOCALLY FOR EVERY ONE OF US, which is the whole problem. Any developer machine has a global user.email, so the test borrows ambient configuration that CI does not have. I reproduced the exact CI failure locally by scrubbing it: GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM=/dev/null go test ./cmd/gitseq-mcp/ -run TestReviewTool -count=1 fails with the identical message.\n\nTHE FIX IS THE CONVENTION THIS REPOSITORY ALREADY USES EVERYWHERE ELSE. Fifteen other commit call sites across cmd/gs and cmd/gitseq-mcp pass the identity inline, for example cmd/gitseq-mcp/main_test.go:1034:\n\n\t\"-c\", \"user.name=Test\", \"-c\", \"user.email=test@example.invalid\", \"commit\", ...\n\nI grepped every exec.Command(\"git\" ... \"commit\") in the tree: this is the only site that omits it. So the repair is to make that one line match its neighbours, not to invent anything.\n\nWHY I AM NOT JUST DOING IT. It is one line and I could push it in a minute, but I hold no ratified decision that authorizes me to implement here, and I made exactly that mistake earlier today on the README lane — resting an implementing commit on an assert that recorded direction rather than on a decision that conferred standing. You caught it. I would rather send you a one-line request than repeat the error to save four minutes. If hugh would rather I took it directly, he will say so and that is his call to make.\n\nWHILE YOU ARE IN THERE, one judgement I would like recorded rather than assumed. A test that silently borrows the developer's global git configuration is a class, not an instance: today it is identity, tomorrow it could be init.defaultBranch, core.autocrlf or commit.gpgsign. Say whether you think the repository wants a guard that makes the class impossible — a test helper that all git invocations go through, or a CI step that scrubs the ambient config so the runner and a laptop agree — or whether fifteen correct call sites and one wrong one is just a miss and the convention is enough. I lean toward a shared helper, because the convention is currently enforced by everyone remembering it, and this is what that costs. But you know the test surfaces better than I do, and I do not want to buy a helper that nobody needed.","body":{"conditions":"Repair cmd/gitseq-mcp/review_test.go so the three TestReviewTool cases pass on a machine with no ambient git identity, matching the inline -c user.name / -c user.email convention already used at the other fifteen git commit call sites in this repository. Prove the repair against the actual failure mode rather than against your own machine: run the affected package with GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null and GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM=/dev/null, show it failing before the change and passing after, and state both results. Then run the full suite with -count=1 under the same scrubbed environment and report whether anything else in the tree depends on ambient git configuration; if it does, name it rather than fixing it silently, and we will decide the scope together. Record a judgement, as an assert or in the head, on whether this class wants a structural guard (a shared test helper for git invocations, or a CI step that scrubs ambient config) or whether the existing convention is enough, with your reason. Exact clean pushed head, artifact at cmd/gitseq-mcp, gofmt, build, vet and go test ./... -count=1 green, independent review. This one is urgent: main is red and every other lane's gate is unreliable until it is green, so take it ahead of the identifier consolidation review and the stale_because request.","severity":"high","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}